From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 10:45:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: add a write-only debugfs node that flushes the
 PPE tables

The PPE tables are filled from userspace through the flowtable, but there
is no way to empty them again after probe: airoha_ppe_flush_sram_entries()
runs once from airoha_ppe_init(), and every file under
/sys/kernel/debug/ppe/ is either read-only or a single-value knob.  A
repeated fill experiment therefore starts each round on top of the
previous round's entries: the D2 fill arm's bound cannot be compared
across rounds even though the instrument itself is sound.

The residue covers both halves of the hash space.  The SRAM half holds
hardware entries that can outlive the driver's bookkeeping; the DRAM half
is the coherent buffer the PPE reads its entries from and is never zeroed
again once probe is over.  Clearing only one half leaves
/sys/kernel/debug/ppe/usage counting the other as bound.

Expose /sys/kernel/debug/ppe/flush, write-only (mode 0200).  Writing "1"
returns both tables to their post-probe state:

 - the SRAM half is cleared through the same per-entry control-register
   handshake the probe-time clear uses, with the live geometry, so with
   flow counters in use each PPE contributes its real 4096 entries and no
   write ever names an entry the hardware does not have;
 - the DRAM half is zeroed in the coherent buffer, which is the table the
   PPE walks.

The flush takes flow_offload_mutex so the live geometry cannot change under
it (airoha_ppe_hw_init() reprograms the per-PPE SRAM size once the NPU
accepts flow counters, and it runs under that mutex), and ppe_lock so an
offload cannot land between two commits.  A failed handshake stops the
flush, names the PPE and the entry in the kernel log and returns -EIO;
nothing reports success for a table it did not clear.  Any value other
than "1" is refused with -EINVAL, so a typo cannot clear the hardware in
the middle of an experiment.

The node clears the tables, not the flows.  It does not clear conntrack:
flows offloaded into the flowtable keep their IPS_OFFLOAD bit and their
packets are still handed to the PPE instead of being forwarded in
software, so the node has to be paired with

    nft flush flowtable inet fw4 ft

On its own it turns those flows into silent drops.  It is a manual
experiment knob and must never be driven by a script; nothing calls it
automatically, and a kernel that never writes the file behaves exactly as
before.

The intended order is: stop the flows (netwatchdog hold), flush the
flowtable, write the node, replay the traffic, sample.  Build-tested with
the OpenWrt arm64 7.3 kernel (airoha_ppe.o and airoha_ppe_debugfs.o); the
"bound back to 0" readback needs the board and belongs to the bench.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -19,6 +19,11 @@
 #include "airoha_regs.h"
 #include "airoha_eth.h"
 
+/* The write-only debugfs "flush" node in airoha_ppe_debugfs.c is the only
+ * caller, so the prototype stays here instead of moving to airoha_eth.h.
+ */
+int airoha_ppe_flush_tables(struct airoha_ppe *ppe);
+
 /* Serialize airoha_gdm_dev flags, QDMA pointer and PPE CPU port
  * configuration.
  */
@@ -2019,6 +2024,67 @@ static int airoha_ppe_flush_sram_entries
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* Clear both PPE tables on behalf of the write-only "flush" debugfs node, so
+ * a repeated fill experiment can start from bound 0 instead of inheriting
+ * the previous round's entries.  The range follows the live geometry: when
+ * flow counters are in use each PPE has half of its SRAM, so the loop never
+ * names an entry the hardware does not have.
+ *
+ * The SRAM half needs the per-entry control-register handshake, exactly as
+ * the probe-time clear does.  The DRAM half is the same coherent buffer the
+ * PPE reads its entries from, so zeroing it is the write.  Leaving the DRAM
+ * half alone would keep the flows that hashed there in the table and
+ * /sys/kernel/debug/ppe/usage would still count them as bound - which is
+ * the residue this node exists to remove.
+ *
+ * The two locks have different jobs.  flow_offload_mutex keeps the geometry
+ * from changing between the size computation below and the loop:
+ * airoha_ppe_hw_init() reprograms the per-PPE SRAM size once the NPU accepts
+ * flow counters, and that runs from airoha_ppe_offload_setup() under this
+ * mutex.  ppe_lock keeps a concurrent offload from landing between two
+ * commits.  Neither makes clearing the tables safe with live traffic: run it
+ * with the flows stopped, and re-create them afterwards.
+ *
+ * It clears the tables only.  It does not touch conntrack: flows nft
+ * offloaded into the flowtable keep their IPS_OFFLOAD bit, and their packets
+ * are still handed to the PPE instead of being forwarded in software, so the
+ * caller has to pair this with "nft flush flowtable inet fw4 ft".  On its own
+ * it turns those flows into silent drops.  It is a manual experiment knob
+ * and must never be driven by a script.
+ */
+int airoha_ppe_flush_tables(struct airoha_ppe *ppe)
+{
+	struct airoha_foe_entry *hwe = ppe->foe;
+	u32 sram_num_entries, dram_num_entries, base;
+	int i, err = 0;
+
+	mutex_lock(&flow_offload_mutex);
+
+	sram_num_entries = airoha_ppe_get_total_sram_num_entries(ppe);
+	dram_num_entries = airoha_ppe_get_total_num_entries(ppe) -
+			   sram_num_entries;
+	base = airoha_ppe_get_sram_num_entries(ppe);
+
+	spin_lock_bh(&ppe_lock);
+	for (i = 0; i < sram_num_entries; i++) {
+		memset(&hwe[i], 0, sizeof(*hwe));
+		err = airoha_ppe_foe_commit_sram_entry(ppe, i);
+		if (err) {
+			dev_err(ppe->eth->dev,
+				"PPE%u: SRAM entry %u did not clear: %d; "
+				"the tables may be partially cleared\n",
+				i / base + 1, i % base, err);
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	memset(&hwe[sram_num_entries], 0, dram_num_entries * sizeof(*hwe));
+	spin_unlock_bh(&ppe_lock);
+
+	mutex_unlock(&flow_offload_mutex);
+
+	return err ? -EIO : 0;
+}
+
 static struct airoha_npu *airoha_ppe_npu_get(struct airoha_eth *eth)
 {
 	struct airoha_npu *npu = airoha_npu_get(eth->dev);
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe_debugfs.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe_debugfs.c
@@ -7,6 +7,11 @@
 #include "airoha_eth.h"
 #include "airoha_regs.h"
 
+/* Defined in airoha_ppe.c, which owns the table geometry and the SRAM
+ * control-register handshake; this file only exposes the write trigger.
+ */
+int airoha_ppe_flush_tables(struct airoha_ppe *ppe);
+
 static void airoha_debugfs_ppe_print_tuple(struct seq_file *m,
 					   void *src_addr, void *dest_addr,
 					   u16 *src_port, u16 *dest_port,
@@ -365,9 +370,56 @@ static int airoha_ppe_debugfs_tb_regs_sh
 }
 DEFINE_SHOW_ATTRIBUTE(airoha_ppe_debugfs_tb_regs);
 
-/* The L2B fields in the PPE window: 64 words starting at FE 0xe00.  The
- * ethertype enable mask sits at 0xe8c and the 8-entry ethertype table at
- * 0xed0..0xedc, eight slots in four words.
+/* Write-only (0200) node that returns both PPE tables to the state they have
+ * after probe, so a repeated fill experiment can start from bound 0 again.
+ * It accepts exactly "1"; every other value is refused rather than
+ * interpreted, because a typo must not clear the hardware in the middle of
+ * an experiment.  A read would report nothing useful, so the file has no
+ * read side at all.
+ *
+ * The tables are cleared, not the flows: pair it with
+ * "nft flush flowtable inet fw4 ft" or the conntrack entries that keep their
+ * IPS_OFFLOAD bit will keep sending packets to a table that no longer holds
+ * them.  This is a manual knob and must never be driven by a script.
+ */
+static ssize_t airoha_ppe_debugfs_flush_write(struct file *file,
+					      const char __user *ubuf,
+					      size_t count, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	struct airoha_ppe *ppe = file_inode(file)->i_private;
+	size_t len = count;
+	char buf[8];
+	int err;
+
+	if (len >= sizeof(buf))
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (copy_from_user(buf, ubuf, len))
+		return -EFAULT;
+
+	buf[len] = '\0';
+	if (len && buf[len - 1] == '\n')
+		buf[--len] = '\0';
+	if (strcmp(buf, "1"))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/* The callee prints why and names the PPE whose table did not clear.
+	 * -EIO means the tables may be partially cleared; the caller must not
+	 * sample as if the flush had succeeded.
+	 */
+	err = airoha_ppe_flush_tables(ppe);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
+	return count;
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations airoha_ppe_debugfs_flush_fops = {
+	.write		= airoha_ppe_debugfs_flush_write,
+};
+
+int airoha_ppe_debugfs_init(struct airoha_ppe *ppe)
+{
+	ppe->debugfs_dir = debugfs_create_dir("ppe", NULL);
  */
 #define AIROHA_PPE_L2B_BASE		0x0e00
 #define AIROHA_PPE_L2B_WORDS		64
@@ -433,8 +485,10 @@ int airoha_ppe_debugfs_init(struct airoh
 			    &airoha_ppe_debugfs_bind_rate_fops);
 	debugfs_create_file("tb_regs", 0444, ppe->debugfs_dir, ppe,
 			    &airoha_ppe_debugfs_tb_regs_fops);
-	debugfs_create_file("l2b", 0444, ppe->debugfs_dir, ppe,
-			    &airoha_ppe_debugfs_l2b_fops);
+	debugfs_create_file("flush", 0200, ppe->debugfs_dir, ppe,
+			    &airoha_ppe_debugfs_flush_fops);
 
 	return 0;
 }
+	return 0;
+}
